Short answer: for important/official forms (tax, payroll, bank accounts, government benefits/IDs) your name should match the name on your Social Security record exactly (or very closely). For informal forms it’s usually less strict.

Details and practical tips
- Why it matters: agencies and employers match name + SSN. If the name on the form doesn’t match the name SSA has for that SSN, it can delay processing, trigger verification requests, or cause tax/employment reporting problems.
- What “match” means:
  - Exact legal name (first, middle, last) as it appears on your Social Security card or SSA record is safest.
  - Minor differences sometimes tolerated (presence/absence of a middle initial, using a middle name versus initial), but hyphens, suffixes (Jr./III), or a different last name (maiden vs married) commonly cause mismatches.
  - Accents/diacritics are often ignored in SSA records; don’t rely on them being significant, but keep spelling otherwise identical.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order): update your SSA record first (Form SS-5 plus required ID/documentation) so future forms will match.
- If the recipient needs the SSN now and you can’t change SSA immediately:
  - Provide the name as it appears on your Social Security card if possible.
  - If the form already requires a name that differs, attach a brief note or supporting documentation (e.g., marriage certificate, ID) and tell the recipient you’re in the process of updating SSA records.
- Security reminder: don’t send your full SSN over unsecured email or forms unless you’re sure the site or recipient is legitimate and secure.

If you tell me what kind of form this is (employer, bank, tax, benefits, online account, etc.), I can give more specific advice and help draft a short explanation you can attach or send.

