Short answer: yes — for anything that’s tied to your Social Security Number (tax forms, payroll/I-9/E-Verify, SSA records, certain government benefits) your name should match the name on your Social Security record (your legal name) or you can run into problems. For other, lower-risk forms (some customer account signups, informal forms) small variations are often tolerated, but it depends on the organization.

Details and practical guidance:
- Why exact match matters:
  - The Social Security Administration and the IRS match names to SSNs. If the name on a tax or payroll form doesn’t match the name on SSA records, income reporting can be rejected or delayed and you could face backup withholding or other processing issues.
  - Employers using E-Verify or agencies checking identity will compare the name you give to the SSA record; mismatches can trigger follow-up and delays.
- What counts as a mismatch:
  - Major differences (Bob vs Robert, missing middle name or initial, missing or added suffix like Jr./Sr., different last name after marriage) can cause problems.
  - Small formatting differences (hyphens, apostrophes, spaces) sometimes are okay, but not guaranteed — it varies by system.
- Common examples:
  - If your SSN is under “Robert A. Smith,” using “Bob Smith” on a W-4 or W-9 may cause a mismatch.
  - If your card says “Maria Lopez-Garcia” and you enter “Maria Lopez Garcia” (no hyphen), it may or may not be accepted depending on the receiving system.
- What to do:
  - If the form is for taxes, payroll, benefits, SSA, or other government use: enter your legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.
  - If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order) and you haven’t updated SSA, update your SSA record first (see SSA.gov; form SS-5 and required documents) so future matches succeed.
  - If a small formatting issue is unavoidable on a specific form, contact the organization’s HR or the form’s contact to ask whether the variation is acceptable or whether they need proof/clarification.
  - Keep documentation of any name-change paperwork handy (marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license).
- Security note: don’t share your full SSN in unsecured channels. You already masked it here (good). Only give full SSN to trusted parties and over secure forms or in-person when required.

If you tell me what kind of form this is (employer W-4, bank account, government benefits, etc.) I can give a short, specific recommendation for that situation or help draft a message to the organization if you need to explain a name variation.

