--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 2329 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: generally yes — use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security record (the name associated with your SSN). Small variations are sometimes tolerated, but mismatches can cause verification failures, payroll/tax reporting problems, or delays.

Details and practical advice
- When an exact match is usually required
  - Government forms, SSA verification, employer payroll/tax reporting, background checks, and many banks/financial institutions typically compare the name you give to the name on your Social Security record. If they don’t match, the check can fail.
- Variations that are often acceptable
  - Minor punctuation or spacing differences (e.g., Smith-Jones vs Smith Jones) or inclusion/omission of a middle initial are sometimes tolerated.
- Variations that can cause problems
  - Using a nickname instead of your legal given name (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert”), using a different last name (maiden vs married) when your SSA record hasn’t been updated, or dropping/adding parts of a compound last name.
- If your name on the document doesn’t match your SSA record
  - Ask the organization whether they accept the variation or whether they require the legal name. Many will accept supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order) to explain a different name.
  - If your SSA record is out of date, update it with the Social Security Administration (form SS‑5 or online through my Social Security where available). Bring proof of identity and name-change documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate, etc.).
  - For employers: use the name that matches SSA so your wages are credited properly. If you recently changed your name, update the SSA first or provide documentation to your payroll department as they instruct.
- Practical steps
  - When in doubt, put your legal name as it appears on your Social Security card.
  - If you must use a different name on the form, attach or offer the supporting documentation and check first with the requester.
  - Don’t post or share your full SSN publicly. Only share it directly with trusted, authorized organizations.

If you want, I can:
- Help draft a short message to the organization asking whether the variation they requested is acceptable; or
- Show what documentation you’ll need to update your name with SSA.

