Short answer: For most official uses, yes — the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (your legal name) or at least match exactly enough that the agency can verify it. Small differences in punctuation or using/omitting a middle initial are often tolerated, but different spellings, nicknames, or a different legal last name can cause problems or delays.

More detail and practical guidance:
- When an exact match is important
  - Social Security Administration records (benefits, earnings history)
  - Tax returns and IRS matching (name + SSN must match SSA records to avoid refund delays or notices)
  - Employment verification / Form I-9 and background checks
  - Banking, government IDs, passport, immigration paperwork, and many official certifications
- Minor variations that are usually acceptable
  - Punctuation, spaces, and diacritics (hyphens, apostrophes) are often normalized by systems
  - Inclusion or omission of a middle initial is frequently tolerated
- Variations that commonly cause issues
  - Different legal last name (maiden vs married) if SSA hasn’t been updated
  - Nicknames (e.g., “Jim” vs “James”) in place of your legal name
  - Misspellings or transposed letters that change the name
- What to do if the name doesn’t match your SSA record
  1. Check what’s on your Social Security card and your SSA online account (if you have one).
  2. If the SSA record is wrong or outdated, update it with the SSA before submitting critical forms. You’ll typically need:
     - Proof of identity (U.S. passport, state ID/driver’s license)
     - Proof of legal name change (marriage certificate, court order) if applicable
     - Proof of citizenship/immigration status if required
     - See SSA.gov for exact document requirements and the local office process.
  3. If you can’t update immediately, tell the requester (employer, bank, etc.) you’re correcting your SSA record and ask what temporary documentation they will accept (photo ID + marriage certificate, etc.).
- Timing and impact
  - Fixing SSA records can take days to weeks; IRS matching problems can delay refunds or processing.
  - For employment I-9s, employers can usually accept current ID documents; for taxes, mismatches can trigger IRS notices.

If you want, tell me:
- Which form you’re filling out (employer I-9, tax form, bank/account application, etc.), and
- How your name currently appears on your Social Security card versus how you want it on the form.
I can give more specific advice or draft a short note to the requester explaining the situation.

Note: Don’t share your full SSN here — last four digits are fine for context, but avoid posting the entire number in chat. I see you included only the last four.

