Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — the name on the form should match the name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA) or the organization’s records, because many verifications match your name against the SSN. Minor, common variations are sometimes accepted, but exact matches avoid delays or rejection.

Details and practical steps

- Why it matters
  - Federal, payroll, tax, and identity-verification systems commonly match your SSN to the name on file at the SSA (or the organization’s database). If the names don’t match, the form or transaction can be delayed, flagged, or rejected (for example, IRS reporting, employment verification, background checks, bank / KYC checks).
- What typically counts as a match
  - Exact match to your SSA record is safest.  
  - Minor variations that are often tolerated: presence/absence of a middle initial, inclusion of a middle name instead of initial, punctuation differences (periods, commas), and common spacing/diacritic stripping.  
  - Variations that commonly cause problems: nicknames (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert”), different last names (married vs maiden) unless both are on file, large spelling differences, or inconsistent use of legal suffixes (Jr./Sr./III).
- If the form asks for only the last four digits
  - Many forms request only the last four for identification. Even then, the name you give should generally be the legal name used with SSA or the institution’s records — the last four helps identify the record, but name mismatches can still cause problems.
- What to do if your name on the document doesn’t match SSA records
  1. Read the form’s instructions — some forms specify “legal name” or “name as shown on Social Security card.” Follow that guidance.  
  2. If possible, use the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card.  
  3. If your current legal name differs from SSA records, update SSA (Form SS‑5) before submitting forms that require a match, or ask the recipient whether they accept supporting documentation.  
  4. If you can’t update SSA right away, contact the organization receiving the form, explain the discrepancy, and ask whether they will accept the alternate name with supporting ID. Some accept a note or additional docs; others require an SSA update.  
  5. Avoid sending your full SSN over unsecured email. If you must transmit it, use the organization’s secure upload or follow their secure instructions.
- Quick examples
  - “Jane A. Smith” (SSA) vs “Jane Alice Smith” — usually OK.  
  - “Jane Smith” (SSA) vs “Janie Smith” — likely to cause a problem.  
  - “Jane Smith-Doe” vs “Jane Doe” — could cause a problem unless both names are on file.

One more thing: you wrote “XXX-XX-3456 (last four: 7890)” — that’s inconsistent. If you plan to give a last-four for verification, double-check which four digits are correct before submitting.

If you want, I can:
- Review the exact form instructions if you paste them (do not include your full SSN in the chat), or
- Draft a short message you can send to the form recipient explaining a name/SSN discrepancy.

