Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: in most cases the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches — even small ones — can cause processing delays, rejected paperwork, or problems with tax and employment reporting.

Key points and practical steps

- Why exact matching matters
  - The SSA and IRS match SSNs to names. If the name you give doesn’t match the name on the SSA record, employers, payroll, and the IRS can show a “name/SSN mismatch” and this can cause notices or delay tax/benefit processing.
  - Banks, government agencies, and background-check services often verify identity using the SSN and the legal name. Mismatches can lead to extra identity-verification steps.

- What counts as a mismatch
  - Different last name (maiden vs. married), missing or different middle name/initial, suffixes (Jr., Sr.), hyphens, punctuation, or abbreviated given names can all create mismatches.
  - Minor punctuation differences (apostrophes, hyphens) are sometimes tolerated, but it’s safer to match exactly.

- Recommended actions
  1. Verify the exact name on your Social Security card or by signing into your “my Social Security” account or contacting SSA. Use that exact spelling on forms that ask for SSN.
  2. If the SSA name is wrong or needs updating, correct it with SSA (Form SS-5 and required identity/supporting documents such as a marriage certificate, court order, or passport). Don’t wait to change other records first — update SSA first for tax-year consistency.
  3. If you can’t change the form to match SSA, contact the organization requesting the form and ask what they accept (some accept an “also known as” line or supporting documentation).
  4. Keep copies of any supporting documents you send (marriage certificate, court order, passport, etc.) and note dates of submission.
  5. For employers: give HR/payroll the name exactly as it appears on your SSA record so your W-2 will match your SSN/name.

- Examples
  - If your Social Security card says “Jennifer L. Smith” but the form has “Jenny Smith,” that can trigger a mismatch — better to use “Jennifer L. Smith.”
  - If your legal last name changed by marriage, make sure SSA is updated before relying on the new name for tax filings or payroll.

A couple of extras
- I noticed two different fragments in your message (XXX-XX-3456 vs. last four 7890). Be careful sharing SSNs in messages — don’t post the full SSN publicly. If you want help, you can tell me which form or agency is asking and I can draft a short message you can send them asking whether minor differences are acceptable or what documentation they need.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email you can send to the organization asking about their name-matching rules, or
- Walk you through how to check/update your SSA record?

