Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — in most official situations the name on the form should match the legal name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Small, common formatting differences (middle initial vs full middle name, punctuation, capitalization) are often tolerated, but substantive differences (different last name, different first name, missing legal suffixes) can cause delays or problems.

What can go wrong if the names don’t match
- Processing delays (tax refunds, benefits, background checks).
- Rejection of the form or additional identity verification requests.
- Employer/payroll reporting problems (W‑2s, withholding).
- Mismatches with IRS/SSA records that can trigger notices or require corrections.

When exact match is required
- Tax and government forms (IRS, SSA, SSA benefit paperwork).
- Employer payroll and tax reporting (W‑4/W‑2).
- Applications that depend on your SSN to confirm identity.

When small differences are usually OK
- Minor punctuation, accent marks, capitalization, or using a middle initial instead of the full middle name are often normalized.
- Informal forms or some private companies may accept obvious, minor variants if the SSN and other identifiers match.

What you should do
1. Compare with your Social Security card and most recent SSA record — that is the authoritative spelling.
2. If your name on the SSA record is wrong or out of date (e.g., you changed your name after marriage), update it with SSA before submitting important forms if you can. SSA name changes require proof (ID, proof of name change such as a marriage certificate or court order). Check SSA.gov for the required documents and options to apply.
3. If you can’t change the SSA record first, ask the organization receiving the form whether they will accept the variation and what proof they need (e.g., marriage certificate, driver’s license). Some forms have an “also known as” or “previous name” field — use that.
4. Keep copies of any supporting documents you provide.
5. Never send your full SSN in unsecured messages. (You provided part of it here — avoid sharing the full number in email/chat.)

If you tell me what type of form it is (tax return, employer form, bank account application, government benefit, etc.) I can give more specific guidance on whether minor differences are likely to be acceptable and what to do next.

