Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: it depends on the purpose of the form — but whenever an organization is using your SSN to verify your identity (IRS, SSA, employer I-9, banks/credit checks, background checks), the name on the document should match your legal name as recorded with the Social Security Administration (SSA). Mismatches can cause delays, rejected forms, or extra verification steps.

Practical guidance and common cases
- Exact match required or strongly recommended:
  - SSA and IRS-related uses (tax returns, refunds): the name + SSN must match SSA records.
  - Employer I-9 and government ID checks: use your legal name shown on identity documents.
  - Bank account opening, KYC, credit checks: institutions typically require the name that matches your official ID and/or SSA/credit records.

- Minor differences usually tolerated:
  - Middle name vs. middle initial (John P. Smith vs John Paul Smith) — usually OK.
  - Omitted punctuation, capitalization, or diacritics (O’Neill vs ONeill) — usually OK.
  - Common variations of formatting (Elizabeth A. Smith vs Elizabeth Anne Smith) — often accepted.

- Differences that often cause problems:
  - Nicknames instead of legal name (Liz Smith vs Elizabeth Smith).
  - Different last names because of marriage/divorce if SSA record isn’t updated.
  - Missing or different suffixes when the suffix is on SSA record (Jr., III).
  - Hyphenation or compound-name changes not reflected in SSA.

What to do if the names don’t match
1. Check what name is on your Social Security record:
   - If possible, view your record at SSA (my Social Security) or look at your Social Security card.
2. If your legal name has changed and SSA isn’t updated, update SSA before submitting forms that require verification — you’ll need proof (marriage certificate, court order, divorce decree, passport, state ID).
3. If you can’t update SSA immediately:
   - Contact the organization asking what they accept (some will accept a legal document proving name change).
   - Attach or provide supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license, passport).
4. When signing forms, use your legal name (the one on SSA/ID). If the form has a place for “other names used” or “previous name,” fill that in.

Examples
- Your SSA record: “Rebecca L. Johnson”
  - Acceptable: “Rebecca L Johnson,” “Rebecca Lynn Johnson”
  - Problematic: “Becky Johnson” or “Rebecca Smith” (if not updated)

