Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — in most cases the name on the form should match the name on the Social Security Administration (SSA) records (and on any ID you will use to verify it). Mismatches are a common reason for delays, rejected forms, payroll/tax errors, or extra verification steps.

Details and practical tips
- Why it matters: organizations (employers, banks, government agencies, tax authorities) typically match your name + SSN (or last 4 digits, DOB, etc.) against SSA or their records. If the name and SSN don’t line up, they’ll often pause processing to ask for proof or a correction.
- How exact: exact spelling is best. Small differences sometimes tolerated (use/omission of a middle initial, accents/diacritics, or “Bob” vs “Robert” in informal cases), but you can’t rely on that. Punctuation, transposed letters, or different last names (e.g., due to marriage) are likely to trigger follow-up.
- What to do if names differ:
  - If your name on the document should match your SSA record, either update the SSA record (if your legal name changed) or change the form to match the SSA record.
  - If you can’t update SSA immediately, attach supporting documentation (driver’s license, passport, marriage certificate, court order) and contact the organization to ask how they want to proceed.
  - For employers/HR: tell them you have a name-record mismatch and offer to provide ID or SSA proof; they can advise the specific steps.
- If you recently changed your name: you should update SSA (Form SS-5) so wages/taxes and benefits are reported correctly. Without that, you’ll likely see payroll/tax problems.
- Security reminder: avoid sharing your full SSN in unsecured channels (email, chat). It’s fine to use the last 4 digits for many verifications; don’t post the full number publicly.

Quick template you can send to HR or the form contact
"Hi — I’m filling out [form name]. My name on file with Social Security is [Exact name as SSA]. I want to confirm whether the form needs to match that spelling exactly or if you accept [alternate spelling]. If a correction is needed, please let me know what documentation you require. Thanks."

One more note: I noticed you wrote “XXX-XX-3456 (last four: 7890)” — those last-four digits don’t match. Be careful to verify the correct last four before submitting.

If you tell me which form or organization it is, I can give more specific guidance or draft a message you can send them.

